{"kind":"stack","id":"soils-s1","title":"Soils: rocks and organic matter","theme":{"background":"#f4efe6","foreground":"#2d2a26","font":"Georgia, serif"}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c1","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c1-q","content":"What are the two main things soil is made from?"},{"type":"button","id":"c1-a","label":"Rock and organic matter","why":"Soil is broken rock mixed with decayed plant and animal material.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c1-b","label":"Sand and water","why":"Pupils may think sand is the whole of soil, forgetting the decayed matter.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c1-c","label":"Clay and air","why":"Pupils may remember clay from pottery and forget the organic part.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c2","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c2-t","content":"Weathering"},{"type":"text","id":"c2-e","content":"The slow breaking down of rock into smaller pieces by wind, rain, ice and plant roots."},{"type":"button","id":"c2-n","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c3","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c3-q","content":"Which process slowly breaks rock into tiny pieces that become soil?"},{"type":"button","id":"c3-a","label":"Weathering","why":"Weathering is the slow breakdown of rock into smaller particles.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-b","label":"Condensation","why":"Pupils may confuse it with water vapour turning to liquid, which does not break rock.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-c","label":"Evaporation","why":"Pupils may mix it up with water drying out, which does not change rock.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c4","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c4-q","content":"What is organic matter in soil?"},{"type":"button","id":"c4-a","label":"Decayed plants and animals","why":"Organic matter is dead plant and animal material breaking down.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c4-b","label":"Small stones","why":"Pupils may think of stones, but those are rock fragments, not organic matter.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c4-c","label":"Melted metal","why":"Pupils may guess something unrelated, mixing up soil with materials from a factory.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c5","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c5-t","content":"Humus"},{"type":"text","id":"c5-e","content":"Dark, rich material in soil made when dead plants and animals decay. It helps plants grow."},{"type":"button","id":"c5-n","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c6","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c6-q","content":"You dig a hole in the school garden. The soil is dark and crumbly. What do you do next?"},{"type":"button","id":"c6-a","label":"Look closely for bits of leaves","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c6-b","label":"Look closely for pieces of stone","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c7","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c7-t","content":"The bits of leaves are decayed organic matter. Your soil is rich and will help plants grow."},{"type":"button","id":"c7-n","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c8","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c8-t","content":"The stone pieces are bits of broken rock. Rock is one of the two main parts of soil."},{"type":"button","id":"c8-n","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c9","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c9-q","content":"Where does most of the rock in soil come from?"},{"type":"button","id":"c9-a","label":"Rock slowly broken down by weathering","why":"Weathering breaks large rocks into the small pieces found in soil.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-b","label":"Rock dropped from the sky","why":"Pupils may imagine meteorites, but soil rock forms from local rock breaking down.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-c","label":"Rock made from melted sand","why":"Pupils may mix up rocks with glass, but soil rock comes from weathering.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c10","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c10-q","content":"Which of these is a good sign of rich, healthy soil?"},{"type":"button","id":"c10-a","label":"Plenty of decayed leaves and worms","why":"Worms and decayed leaves show lots of organic matter in healthy soil.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-b","label":"Only large stones","why":"Pupils may think stones make soil fertile, but they add rock, not organic matter.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-c","label":"Only sand, with no plants","why":"Pupils may think sand alone is good soil, forgetting organic matter.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c11","kind":"end","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c11-h","content":"You finished the soil lesson!"},{"type":"text","id":"c11-m","content":"Well done. Soil is made from broken rock and decayed plants and animals."}],"actions":[]}}
